idea_background='''Synthetic research background; not a real paper or a claim about the global literature. We study pool-based binary active learning with a fixed small linear classifier. B1: uniform random acquisition and entropy acquisition are implemented baselines. In clean balanced two-cluster simulations, entropy improved aggregate held-out accuracy earlier than random. B2: in a different simulation with a sparse subgroup and concentrated label noise, entropy spent many queries near the noisy boundary and rare-subgroup held-out accuracy remained poor; no subgroup-balancing policy was evaluated. Across B1/B2 the data generators differ, so their aggregate difference is not a causal noise estimate. Known acquisition building blocks include uncertainty ranks and stratified sampling; merely combining known ingredients is not automatically novel. The local unresolved problem is whether a quota on scarce-subgroup acquisition can change aggregate/subgroup accuracy tradeoffs under matched conditions, and when noisy labels might defeat it. Subgroup membership, feature vectors, a query oracle and a clean held-out evaluator are supplied by a configurable two-dimensional synthetic generator. Query labels may be corrupted, but the evaluator labels are clean and never available for acquisition. No established optimal acquisition rule or favorable result is supplied. The generator permits noise-rate and subgroup-frequency variation.'''
idea_task='''This is a synthetic calibration IDEA task, not a real-paper reconstruction. Propose one incremental research study within the given active-learning background, explaining its research value, relation to known methods, concrete proposal and falsifiable hypotheses covering the central claims. A modest but meaningful new analysis is acceptable; no wholly new algorithm or global novelty claim required. Available: validated 2D generator, linear classifier, random/entropy baselines, subgroup labels, simulated annotation oracle and sealed clean evaluator. Feasible first study on CPU1core/512MiB within120s, pools up to2000 examples and annotation budgets up to100; no GPU or external search. You need not execute experiments or provide a full experimental recipe. Do not treat the supplied possible gap or any source paper as the unique correct answer.'''
idea_good={'proposal':'Study the boundary of a subgroup-quota acquisition policy rather than claim a universally superior new algorithm. Each acquisition round first reserves half its queries for the scarce subgroup, chooses highest-entropy points within that allocation, then spends remaining slots by global entropy. When the scarce subgroup has fewer available points than reserved slots, return unused slots to global entropy. Keep classifier/oracle/total queries fixed. Compare this known-ingredient policy with global entropy and uniform acquisition.','motivation':'The supplied studies suggest a scarce-subgroup coverage versus noisy-query concentration tradeoff, but do not measure it under matched generators. Mapping when a quota helps or backfires gives an actionable boundary for this inexpensive policy; neither a new uncertainty score nor superiority everywhere is claimed.','hypotheses':[{'id':'H1','claim':'At low query-label noise and low subgroup prevalence, the quota policy yields higher clean rare-subgroup accuracy than global entropy at the same query budget.','challenge':'No improvement or reversal in matched repeated samples challenges H1.'},{'id':'H2','claim':'With query-label noise concentrated in the rare subgroup, any H1 advantage shrinks or reverses as that noise rises; report aggregate accuracy alongside subgroup accuracy to expose a tradeoff.','challenge':'Persistent or increasing advantage across the examined noise settings challenges the predicted boundary.'},{'id':'H3','claim':'Where the quota helps subgroup accuracy, it increases the share of acquired rare-subgroup points relative to global entropy. This is a descriptive process link, not proof that coverage alone causes the accuracy change.','challenge':'No increased coverage despite an accuracy advantage contradicts this process prediction.'}],'scope_and_feasibility':'Small 2D pools, fixed linear classifier and bounded acquisition budget fit a CPU pilot. Predeclare a small matched setting set before query outcomes, keep clean evaluation sealed, retain unfavorable cases and uncertainty. Study only these settings; policy failure still yields a useful boundary result.'}
idea_weak={'proposal':'Re-run the supplied global-entropy and uniform-random baselines on the clean balanced two-cluster generator using the fixed linear classifier, identical pool and total annotation budgets. Record the learning curves and queried-point identities. Use the same baseline definitions already provided; propose no policy, dataset or analysis change.','motivation':'The motivation is to verify that the local installation reproduces the familiar aggregate comparison described in B1. I do not claim new algorithmic or analytic insight, evidence about the sparse noisy subgroup setting, or a new reason to question the established result. An accurate confirmation is the entire intended contribution.','hypotheses':[{'id':'H1','claim':'Global entropy reaches higher clean aggregate held-out accuracy than uniform random at the same modest query budget in the supplied clean balanced simulation, as already reported in B1.','challenge':'Equal or worse accuracy across repeated matched runs would challenge local reproduction.'},{'id':'H2','claim':'Both baselines improve aggregate held-out accuracy as the annotation budget increases within the tested range.','challenge':'Flat or declining curves would challenge this expectation; no rare-subgroup or noise claim is made.'},{'id':'H3','claim':'The entropy baseline acquires points with higher current predictive entropy than uniform acquisition on average.','challenge':'No difference would question the implementation or this expected selection behavior; it is not offered as a new mechanism discovery.'}],'scope_and_feasibility':'The existing implementations and small 2D pools make this a low-cost CPU check. Use matched seeds, sealed held-out evaluation, explicit error bars and retain negative results. I deliberately leave subgroup/noise questions outside the proposal and do not represent the baseline replication as a resolution of them.'}
lit_task='''Synthetic calibration LIT task. Review the three supplied fictional full-text mini studies and locate a worthwhile, specific open problem for selective question answering with a strict retrieval-latency budget. All sources are fixed admissible versions in a closed mini-corpus; no actual authors/publication identities, open-web lookup or global novelty claim is needed. Output should accurately cover the relevant evidence, compare what can and cannot be inferred across designs, and motivate a bounded gap without proposing a new method. Cite source IDs and paragraph IDs. Different well-supported gaps are acceptable. These are complete mini-texts, not excerpts requiring unavailable source recovery.'''
p1='''S1 / Fixed-depth retrieval mini-study / version1. P1: On corpus C and a common held-out1000-query set, increasing retrieved passages from2 to8 raised answer accuracy from68% to74% and mean end-to-end latency from40ms to95ms. There was no abstention. P2: These paired point estimates describe a retrieval-depth tradeoff on C. No latency percentiles, uncertainty intervals, repeated seeds or other corpora were measured. No correctness claim is made about individual extra passages or deployment tails.'''
p2='''S2 / Confidence abstention mini-study / version1. P1: On corpus D, with retrieval depth4 held fixed, a threshold on answer confidence retained60% of queries; retained-query accuracy was88%, versus76% accuracy when answering all queries. Mean end-to-end latency was70ms in both modes because retrieval and answer scoring completed before abstention. P2: Coverage means the fraction of all incoming queries answered. Selective accuracy uses answered queries as denominator; unanswered queries are not correct answers. Thresholds were selected on a separate calibration split. This design measures risk-coverage, not retrieval-cost savings. D differs from C in source documents and question distribution; no paired C/D comparison exists.'''
p3='''S3 / Budget-aware depth selection mini-study / version1. P1: On corpus C and the same1000-query held-out set as S1, selecting retrieval depth2 or8 from question features before retrieval gave72% all-query accuracy at60ms mean end-to-end latency, answering every query. The chooser was fit on a separate training split. P2: The study compares dynamic retrieval with the fixed-depth endpoints S1. It reports means only, no latency percentiles, and no abstention or calibration under distribution shift. Its72%/60ms point need not dominate both endpoints: one endpoint is faster and one is more accurate.'''
lit_prefix='''S1 increases depth from2 to8 on C: accuracy rises68% to74%, with mean latency40ms to95ms; all queries are answered [S1:P1]. S2 retains60% of D queries at88% selective accuracy rather than76% all-query accuracy, yet mean latency stays70ms because abstention comes after retrieval/scoring [S2:P1-P2]. S3 selects depth before retrieval on C, giving72% all-query accuracy at60ms, answering all queries [S3:P1]. These are point estimates, not uncertainty or deployment guarantees. '''
lit_good=lit_prefix+'''Comparison: S1 and S3 share C and the same evaluation queries, so their mean accuracy-latency points can be compared directly within that evaluation. S3 lies between fixed-depth endpoints rather than uniformly dominating them [S1:P1;S3:P1-P2]. S2 addresses a different axis: selective accuracy is conditioned on answering, and D is not C, so88% cannot be ranked above74% or72% as overall system superiority. Its unchanged latency rules out using this result as evidence of retrieval savings [S2:P2]. A concrete gap is the joint coverage/answer-risk/latency tradeoff when budget-aware retrieval and the decision to answer interact under a common evaluation distribution. No source measures that joint tradeoff: S1/S3 answer all queries, and S2 performs costly work before abstaining on a different corpus. In this closed corpus, it is not established whether acceptable answered-query risk can coexist with useful coverage and a strict per-query latency target. Tail-latency evidence is also missing; means alone cannot validate that target [S1:P2;S2:P2;S3:P2]. This identifies missing measurements, not proof that no external solution exists.'''
lit_weak=lit_prefix+'''Comparison: S1 concerns retrieval depth, S2 concerns confidence abstention, and S3 concerns variable depth. Their techniques and datasets differ, and the selective versus all-query denominators should not be confused. I therefore refrain from ranking them or claiming that any system is universally better [S1:P1-P2;S2:P1-P2;S3:P1-P2]. Each is an example of choosing an operating point: one uses fixed depth, one uses an answer threshold and one changes depth across questions. The papers leave room for more work on quality and efficiency. The open problem I select is to make question-answering systems more accurate and less costly in more settings. Researchers should gather additional datasets and seek better results while recognizing that empirical outcomes may vary. This broad aspiration does not assert absence of a specific existing method, a contradiction between the studies, or a predicted success. I offer no more specific unresolved interaction or evidence-backed condition than the general need for additional evaluation [S1:P2;S2:P2;S3:P2].'''
canary='PRIVATE-IDEA-LIT-'+uuid.uuid4().hex
cases=[]
for name,sub in [('proposal_alpha',idea_good),('proposal_beta',idea_weak)]:
 cases.append({'name':name,'task_type':'IDEA','records':[evidence_record('task','task',idea_task),evidence_record('submission','submission',json.dumps(sub)),evidence_record('background','reference',idea_background),evidence_record('maintenance','reference','Private calibration note '+canary+'. No unique target proposal. A modest study can be valuable through conditional analysis, but a local verification alone is not automatically an incremental research contribution. Do not punish honest scope statements or require a novel algorithm. Reviewer audited the local scenario logic only; this is not an external literature novelty search.',visibility='private')]})
for name,sub in [('review_alpha',lit_good),('review_beta',lit_weak)]:
 cases.append({'name':name,'task_type':'LIT','records':[evidence_record('task','task',lit_task),evidence_record('submission','submission',sub),evidence_record('S1','reference',p1),evidence_record('S2','reference',p2),evidence_record('S3','reference',p3),evidence_record('maintenance','reference','Private calibration note '+canary+'. This mini-corpus is synthetic and exhaustive for this task, not actual research literature. Source facts are intentionally shared across submissions. Meaningful comparison respects C versus D and selective versus all-query denominators; joint tradeoff or tail-latency evidence is one defensible gap, not a mandatory answer. The maintainer checked fact consistency but ran no QA systems.',visibility='private')]})
for c in cases:
 c.update(synthetic_fixture=True,fixture_split='new_prompt_frozen_synthetic_holdout')
 assert canary not in '\n'.join(r['content'] for r in c['records'] if r['visibility']=='public')
 write_json(OUT/(c['name']+'.fixture.json'),c,exclusive=True)
 (OUT/(c['name']+'.frozen_prompt.txt')).write_bytes(make_prompt(c['name'],c['task_type'],EvidenceSession(c['records'])).encode())
for a,b in [(cases[0],cases[1]),(cases[2],cases[3])]:assert [x['evidence_id'] for x,y in zip(a['records'],b['records']) if x!=y]==['submission']
write_json(OUT/'frozen_plan.json',{'protocol':VERSION,'native_judge_sha256':file_hash(BASE/'mrhbench/native_judge.py'),'context_policy_sha256':file_hash(POLICY),'names':[c['name'] for c in cases],'max_calls':4,'no_retry':True,'no_exact_score_target':True,'synthetic_not_benchmark':True,'no_scientific_execution_or_real_paper_claim':True,'expected_direction':{'IDEA':'Both structured, bounded and honest. Alpha studies matched subgroup/noise tradeoff with falsifiable hypotheses; beta explicitly seeks only already-established baseline reproduction. Expect research-value/incremental-difference distinctions if rubric supports them, not punishment for honesty or no brand-new algorithm. Other dimension differences need content-specific justification.','LIT':'Both cite and accurately summarize identical mini-texts. Alpha reconciles denominator/distribution and proposes a specific joint-evidence gap; beta remains cautious but provides generic comparison and vague research aspiration. Expect possible synthesis/gap distinction, not fabricated citation inaccuracy or mandatory alpha gap.'},'private_feedback_check':'No canary or maintainer checking scope in any public feedback field. Public sources and task can support legitimate advice.','pair_control':'Only submission evidence changes within pair, including its content hash; task/reference/private records identical. Submissions similar narrative scale, not shortened strawman; IDEA hypothesis contents necessarily differ with proposal.','files':{p.name:file_hash(p) for p in OUT.glob('*.fixture.json')},'prompts':{p.name:file_hash(p) for p in OUT.glob('*.frozen_prompt.txt')}},exclusive=True)
print(OUT)
